ke is  disruption if the government doesn't  understand that it doesn't understand  anything so nobody wants to see strikes  take place but if the government isn't  willing to really appreciate why they  are taking place I'm afraid  there won't be a way to resolve them and  we've seen actually you know as you said  there's more hope in the railways a  little bit more of an acknowledgment  left in the government that they've got  to do something let's have some more of  that across the economy as a whole  so even during exam time is acceptable  well there will never be a time when  strike action in education isn't  disruptive to young people who are  studying you know there is no easy time  as it were to go on strike and yes it  will have a an impact on young people  there's no doubt about that but frankly  that the solution to that is rather than  condemning workers who feel driven to  take this action the solution to that is  getting down to negotiate and frankly we  haven't seen enough of that and the  reason for that in my view is that the  government are frankly embarrassed at  the state of Public Services after 13  years in power and they see in the  variety of industrial actions taking  place a chance to somehow use it as an  alibi to switch the blame onto workers  themselves to somehow say that's the  reason Public Services aren't performing  most people in this country will know  even when there isn't strike action  taking place a lot of Public Services  are not running to the degree that they  should do with the expectations people  have of them so let's not get into  condemning people let's resolve these  disputes and they won't take place let's  um talk about our beloved NHS should we  Your Leader yesterday saying the NHS  needs reform we're being told that part  of that could be the ditching of a GP  contracts I'm reading it in the paper  this morning that could cost  um an extra seven billion pounds well is  that really something that you want to  embark on at this stage  well what we want okay is a long-term  10-year plan for the NHS that addresses  the fact that it isn't working because  people wanted to work at the moment part  of our plans are for substantial extra  resources abolishing that the non-dong  tax regime for the super rich and using  that to for instance double the number  of doctors being trained but we've also  got to look at new ways of doing things  it's a consultation we're not being  prescriptive I think we've got to be  willing to look at new ways of doing  things like the NHS has always evolved  throughout its history specifically on  the issue rays of you know GPS becoming  direct employees of the NHS rather than  the partnership model there are already  and have been in the last few years  substantial changes that have taken  place to that model a lot of younger  Medics don't want to become GPS on the  old model we've seen sort of big  Healthcare interests come in and buy up  some GP practices so there is change  happening in this area so we've got to  look at that because it's happening  let's look at the best way of doing that  work out how we should do that in  conjunction with staff themselves and  then any costs she will be part of the  equation but the idea that we should  never look at doing things in a  different way or seeing if there's  improvements that can be made I think  that would be a mistake of course more  resources are required but we shouldn't  rule out reform of how we do things as  well okay um your boss seemed to get  himself in quite a pickle yesterday when  he was talking about patients making  self uh referrals  um do you think that's a good idea  I think it already exists in some uh  forms of treatment I think again if  we're having a consultation why not look  at uh how that could play a bigger role  not for everything clearly but for some  areas I think it could one of the real  problems I see quite regularly is the  the struggle tick to get to see a GP  because they're so uh under-resost and  because the capacity isn't there you've  got to obviously get in with a phone  call early in the morning that does put  more pressure on a e as more and more  people simply go straight to a e because  they don't feel they can get to see  their GP to do that so again if there's  different ways we can do that release  some of that pressure as a whole I think  everyone agrees you know as a National  Health System the NHS were in a position  too often where we're spending too much  on hospital care we're not getting in  with the treatments people need before  they get to that acute stage and again  if you're having a consultation about  the long-term future of the NHS in a  public model delivered in a public way  but looking at how it interacts with  patients again let's look at also new  ways of doing things as well as getting  more capacity and resources into it just  to be excuse me just to be absolutely  clear what does labor think at the top  table think we should do if we think we  have internal bleeding  I'm not going to get into specific  treatments and how they should be dealt  with this is the point you did yesterday  well look I think the best way forward  on this is to say we're looking at  different ways of how the NHS can work  we want to do a consultation with  patients with with GPS is the best way  to do that there are some treatments  where you can go immediately to  Specialists already we're looking at the  evidence around that but I think we've  got to address this this issue where so  much pressure is put into a knees as  people turn up presenting there because  they can't get to Access healthcare at  an earlier stage when it might be more  amenable to do that so I don't think we  should ever be in a position where we  are reluctant to talk about how we can  change a public service as long as it's  delivered in public hands in a public  way how is it interacting with patients  how can we do that better we shouldn't